# Udemy Transcript + Textbook Documentation

When this rule is applied or the user provides a Udemy lecture URL (or has one open), do **all** of these every time:

1. Get the **transcript** from `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/notes/` if it already exists; otherwise extract it from the Udemy player and save it there
2. Get the **course images** from `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/images/<sequence>/` if that folder has stills; otherwise play the lecture in the browser and capture stills with **CodeSnap**
3. Write **textbook documentation** to `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/`
4. Add or update the lecture link on the landing page `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`
5. **Git commit** the lecture files in the `Personal` repo (see **E. Commit**) so the user can `git push` without committing themselves

Default course player: [https://www.udemy.com/course/aws-certified-solutions-architect-associate-hands-on/learn/](https://www.udemy.com/course/aws-certified-solutions-architect-associate-hands-on/learn/). Prefer a lecture URL the user gave (or an already-open tab). If you only have a sequence/title, open that player and click the matching item in **Course content**.

Use `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/sample.md` for **writing style** (novice prose, `- [x]` bullets, exam-style Q&A). Do **not** copy its old collapsible **Terminal Commands** / **Code** layout. Do **not** write lecture content into `01.md`. `01.md` is only the landing page + table of contents. Manual archive is `Personal/Files/aws/old.md`.

If a transcript fetch or image capture fails, **troubleshoot first** (login, wrong lecture, closed panel, player not started, CodeSnap command). If it still fails, **stop and tell the user** what you tried and what they need to do. Do not write a lecture page that invents figures or quotes a transcript you never saved.

## A. Get the transcript

1. Look in `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/notes/` for `<sequence>-<Hyphenated-Title>.txt` (or the file the user attached). If it exists and has cue lines under the header, **use it** and skip the player scrape.
2. If that file is missing or empty, open the lecture on Udemy (`browser_navigate` to the lecture URL, or the default `/learn/` player then the matching **Course content** row).
3. Snapshot the page (`browser_snapshot`).
4. Open the **Transcript** panel if it is closed (transcript toggle / `data-purpose="transcript-toggle"`).
5. Collect cue lines in order (`role: button` names between video controls and **Autoscroll**).
6. Get the lecture title and **sequence number** from course content (example: `Play 2. The SAA-C03 Exam` -> `2`).
7. Save `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/notes/<sequence>-<Hyphenated-Title>.txt`:

```
Lecture: <title>
URL: <url>
============================================================

<transcript lines>
```

Filename rules: spaces to `-`, keep readable Title-Case, drop special characters except hyphens.

### Transcript errors

Retry once after a snapshot. Then tell the user, do not guess the lecture text:

- Login / paywall / "Log in to continue": ask them to sign into Udemy in the Cursor browser and re-run.
- Transcript toggle missing (article, quiz, practice test): say the lecture has no transcript panel.
- Empty cue list with the panel open: scroll the transcript pane, wait for cues to load, retry; if still empty, stop.
- Wrong lecture: confirm sequence + title in **Course content** before saving.

## B. Get the course images

1. Look in `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/images/<sequence>/`.
2. If that folder has stills, **use them**. Keep the user’s filenames (lecture 3 style: `1.png`, `2.png`, …).
3. If that folder is missing or empty, **do not stop**. Open the same lecture in the Udemy player (default `/learn/` URL or the lecture URL) and capture stills as follows.

### Capture stills when the folder is empty

Play the section video and snap each distinct slide, diagram, or console screen:

1. Open the lecture, wait for the player, click **Play**. Pause on each new slide or UI (title card, diagram, AWS console, comparison table). Skip talking-head-only frames that add no diagram.
2. Prefer **CodeSnap** (`CodeSnap 📸` / `codesnap.start`) to capture the visible lecture frame. Save each PNG as `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/images/<sequence>/N.png` (`1.png`, `2.png`, …).
3. If CodeSnap cannot target the browser player, fall back to `browser_take_screenshot` of the **video player** (not the whole Udemy chrome). Save with the same `N.png` names.
4. Discard black, blank, or duplicate frames. If a seek-preview is black, press Play, wait until the picture is visible, then pause and snap again.
5. Use **every** saved image. Embed each one next to the transcript content it illustrates.
6. From the lecture `.md` file in `udemy/md/`, the path is `../images/<sequence>/<filename>` (example: `../images/3/1.png`).
7. Caption as **Figure N.** plus a short description of what the slide or UI actually shows. Do not invent extra figures that are not in the folder.

### Image errors

Troubleshoot, then tell the user if stills cannot be saved:

- Player will not start: click the video, Play, wait for buffering; if a login modal appears, ask the user to sign in.
- CodeSnap command missing or does nothing: fall back to `browser_take_screenshot` of the player and say you did.
- Every frame is black after play + pause: DRM/seek preview failed; stop and ask the user to drop extracted stills in `udemy/images/<sequence>/`.
- Wrong lecture playing: switch via **Course content** and recapture.

Example for lecture 3:

- `../images/3/1.png` — title slide
- `../images/3/2.png` — Free Tier vs Sandbox comparison
- `../images/3/3.png` — Challenge Labs website
- `../images/3/4.png` — Challenge Lab console + instructions

## C. Write textbook documentation

Create `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/<sequence>-<Hyphenated-Title>.md`. Every lecture page has **exactly these sections**, in this order:

1. A **back link** to the course table of contents as the **first line**: `[← Course contents](../../01.md)` (from `udemy/md/` that is `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`)
2. Title heading with lecture number
3. Course + lecture links + transcript path
4. **Introduction** (prose a novice can follow)
5. **Detailed Explanation** grouped into steps (see below)
6. **Lab** (a real walkthrough for HOL/console lessons; for theory lessons, say there is no console lab and keep any study snippets here too)
7. **Questions and Answers** (exam-focused; keep each **Answer** in a collapsible `<details>`)
8. **Summary**
9. **References** (Udemy lecture, official AWS docs, transcript file)

Do **not** create standalone **Terminal Commands** or **Code** sections.

### Which sections are accordions

Keep these headings **open** (no outer `<details>`): **Introduction**, **Detailed Explanation**, **Summary**, **References**.

Wrap these in `<details>` accordions:

- Each **Step** inside Detailed Explanation (one accordion per step)
- The whole **Lab** section
- The whole **Questions and Answers** section (individual **Answer** blocks stay nested `<details>` inside it)

Do **not** wrap command or code snippets in their own nested accordions. They stay visible inside the Step or Lab that uses them.

### Detailed Explanation must use the Step format

This is the house style for **every** lecture page. Never write `## Detailed Explanation` as one flat list of bullets.

1. Group the transcript's sub-topics into **4–7 steps** (up to ~10 for a very long cram/cheat-sheet lecture).
2. Head each group with `### Step N — <short descriptive title>`, using an em dash `—` with a space on each side, sentence case, no trailing period.
3. Steps run `1..N` in transcript order, with no gaps or duplicates.
4. Put **each** step in its own accordion: `<details>` / `<summary>Step N — title</summary>` / the `### Step N — title` heading and its body / `</details>`.
5. Under each step, keep the `- [x]` bullets (and their nested children) taken from the transcript.
6. Place a figure inside the step whose bullets it illustrates, after those bullets.

Example shape:

```
## Detailed Explanation

<details>
  <summary>Step 1 — Place SAA-C03 on the AWS certification ladder</summary>

### Step 1 — Place SAA-C03 on the AWS certification ladder

- [x] **What SAA-C03 is**
  - Exam code for **AWS Certified Solutions Architect Associate**.

</details>

<details>
  <summary>Step 2 — Learn the logistics: time, questions, and exam-day tactics</summary>

### Step 2 — Learn the logistics: time, questions, and exam-day tactics

- [x] **Length, question count, and style**
  - **130 minutes** and **65 questions**.

</details>
```

Lab and Q&A use the same accordion pattern:

```
<details>
  <summary>Lab</summary>

## Lab

...

</details>

<details>
  <summary>Questions and Answers</summary>

## Questions and Answers

### Question 1: ...

<details>
<summary>Answer</summary>

- [x] ...

</details>

</details>
```

Reference pages: `udemy/md/1-Welcome-and-How-to-Use-this-Course.md`, `udemy/md/2-The-SAA-C03-Exam.md`, `udemy/md/3-Hands-On-Practice-Free-Tier-vs-Sandbox.md`.

### Terminal commands and code live in the step and the lab

Never isolate commands or snippets in their own section. When a lecture uses a CLI command, JSON policy, ARN, URL, or other snippet:

1. Put the fenced block **inside the Detailed Explanation step** that teaches it (after the bullets that introduce it).
2. Put the **same** snippet again **inside the Lab** task (or Lab overview, for theory lessons) that actually uses it.

Show the snippet inline. Do **not** hide it behind a nested `<details>` accordion. If a lesson has no commands and no code, write none — do not add placeholder `# No commands in this topic` blocks.

For HOL/console lessons, write a real step-by-step tutorial a beginner can repeat. For theory lessons, say there is no console lab and keep Q&A exam-focused.

## D. Update the landing page

In `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`, add the new lecture under the correct section in **Table of contents**:

```
N. [Lecture Title](udemy/md/N-Hyphenated-Title.md)
```

Do not duplicate an existing TOC row. Do not put full lecture notes in `01.md`.

## E. Commit (every request / task)

At the **end of every request** that applies this rule (a new lecture page, a redo, a rule tweak, or any other task under this rule), create a **git commit** in `Personal` (`C:/Users/omeat/Downloads/GITHUB/Personal`). The user will **push**. Do **not** `git push` unless they ask.

1. Run `git status`, `git diff`, and `git log -8 --oneline` in `Personal` before staging.
2. Stage only the files this task changed (typically `Files/aws/udemy/notes/`, `Files/aws/udemy/images/<sequence>/`, `Files/aws/udemy/md/`, `Files/aws/01.md`, and this rule file). Do **not** stage `.env`, credentials, or unrelated dirty files.
3. Commit with a **HEREDOC** message (no `git commit -i`, no `--no-verify`, no amend, no git config).
4. Run `git status` after the commit and tell the user the **commit hash + message**.

### Commit message

Use the **section / lecture topic**, not a generic `Updates` message. Match existing style in this repo (`24-Using-user-Data-and-Metadata`):

- Lecture docs: `N-Hyphenated-Title` (same as the `.md` filename without extension). Example: `25-Access-Keys-and-IAM-Roles-with-EC2`
- Non-lecture work under this rule (this file, TOC-only, tooling): a short topic slug or imperative line. Example: `udemy-transcript-auto-commit`

If the working tree is already clean, skip the commit and say so. If the lecture page was not written because transcript/images failed, **do not** commit a partial invented page.

## Confirm

Tell the user: transcript path + line count (and whether it came from `notes/` or was scraped from Udemy), doc path, figure list from `udemy/images/<sequence>/` (and whether those stills were already on disk or captured with CodeSnap / player screenshot), that `01.md` was updated, and the **git commit hash + message** (or that there was nothing to commit). If anything failed after troubleshooting, list that instead of pretending the lecture page is complete.

